A||||3||V8Fiero||Idle Air Control Valve?||||1||
Z||000000||V8Fiero||07-23-2000||12:12 AM||||I just did the diagnostic check on my 85 SE. It said to that my IAC valve was bad or should be checked. Any way to check to see if these things are working before spending the dough on a new one?What exactly does this valve do anyway? The car does seem to miss every now and then. Could this be the cause of this? Thanks for any help.<BR>Scott<BR>P.S. Its on a 4cyl.||||reg||1
Z||000001||ur2biteme||07-23-2000||01:00 AM||||that was a common code to set on my car a while back.  that and oxygen sensors.  i'd recommend replacing it (if i remember right, its only $20-$25) even if you find it's not the entire problem.  this code usually sets because of other problems, but replacing it is preventative maintenence.  The code sets if the idle is 50 or more rpm above or below where the ecm says it should be....  <P>The IAC is a valve that controls part of the air fuel mixture at idle (hence idle air control valve) keeping it at ideal.  it and the TPS (i think, don't take my word for it) and a few other things keep the idle constant under load changes, example: a/c comming on, on my car with bad grounding-the lights or cooling fan comming on, etc.  these put a slight load on the engine at idle, and this part helps to maintain the "650-750 rpm" idle (mine is usually 850-1000 and rough as hell, but splitfires and a more free flowing exhaust caused that. more low end though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)<P>what i found is that this sensor going bad, or the code for it setting, usually has to do with something else going on, like a bound up exhaust, choked up intake, bad cat converter, vacuum lines, etc.  mine was a muffler.  <P>more than likely the valve isn't bad, there is probably something along the lines of what i allready mentioned.  if you are idling at about 1200-1500, check your vacuum lines.  mine come loose from time to time, so i'm used to it.  if you are idling at about 3000-3500, exhaust manifold is glowing red, then the iac is shot.  <P>sorry about the leingth, hopefully this will help....<p>[This message has been edited by ur2biteme (edited 07-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierospeeder||07-23-2000||05:41 AM||||its a small solenoid that moves a cone in and out of an air passage.  In turn, it lets air to go into the engine to let it idle.  If the idle is going down in rpms, it will open up.<P>Is your car missing while driving or at idle?<BR>You can also have someone turn the key, and have the aircleaner off, and see if the cone is moving.  Check your plug wires for cracks.  Some places the IAC may be an expensive part.  Call around and ask.  If you have the haynes book, i think you can do an ohm test with a multimeter to see if the iac is bad.||||reg||1
Z||000003||V8Fiero||07-23-2000||01:04 PM||||HEY GUYS!!! oops didnt mean to yell. Found my problem. <BR>After reading your replys I remembered my cruise control wasnt working right either. It would slowly loose speed. I check my vacuum lines and sure enough. A teeny tiny crack. Small piece of electrical tape and I was good to go. Thanks a bunch. Glad I didnt run out and buy a new valve.||||reg||1
